    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

## <h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">Lesson Identification Table</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> It's Our Garden.</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> The Pink Flowers!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Catch the main idea of a short, simple story or announcement with visual support or illustrations. Interactive: Respond to simple conversations on familiar topics by using gestures or by pointing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can recognize nature-related words like "tree," "flowers," and "bushes" in simple conversations or stories (e.g., "The tree is tall." "The flowers are pink."). Can follow basic instructions related to outdoor environments in conversations (e.g., "Look at the tall tree."). Can respond to questions about nature by pointing or using gestures (e.g., pointing to a tree in response to the question "Do you see a tree?").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

## <h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows a large picture of a colorful garden or brings in real simple plants/flowers (realia). The teacher points to different parts of the garden. "Look! What is this? It's a garden!" (Using /g/ pronunciation). The teacher then asks students, "Do you like gardens?" (Students show thumbs up/down or nod).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher uses flashcards for "garden," "flower," "tree," "plant," "pink," "green," "tall," "short," "sun." The teacher holds up each card, says the word clearly (e.g., "This is a flower. A pink flower!"), and models the pronunciation, especially /g/ in "garden" and /t/ in "tree," and /s/ in "sun." The teacher uses TPR by miming "tall" (stretching up) and "short" (crouching down) when saying "tall tree" and "short plant." Students listen and point to the corresponding flashcard or realia as the teacher says the words. The teacher then says simple phrases like "The flower is pink" (pointing) and "The tree is tall" (gesturing).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a picture of a flower) "Is this a tree?" (Expected student response: Shake head / "No").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a pink flower) "What color is the flower?" (Expected student response: Point to pink / "Pink").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "It's Our Garden."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, simple story read aloud by the teacher with large, clear illustrations (e.g., a big book, projected images) titled "My Pretty Garden." The story will feature the target vocabulary: "garden," "flower," "tree," "plant," "pink," "green," "tall," "short," "sun," and "grow." Example sentence structures: "This is my garden. The flowers are pink. A tall tree grows here. The sun is big." The teacher plays a short, simple song about a garden (e.g., "The Garden Song" or "Pink Flowers Everywhere") twice, encouraging students to listen for familiar words and follow along with gestures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Receptive: Catch the main idea of a short, simple story or announcement with visual support or illustrations. Interactive: Respond to simple conversations on familiar topics by using gestures or by pointing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Listening:</b> After reading the story, the teacher asks, "What is the story about? A house or a garden?" (Students point to a "garden" flashcard or picture).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Pointing):</b> The teacher makes statements based on the story, and students point to "yes" (thumbs up) or "no" (thumbs down) images. Examples: "The flowers are pink." (True). "The tree is short." (False, if story mentioned a tall tree).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen and Point:</b> As the teacher re-reads key sentences from the story, students listen for target vocabulary (e.g., "tree," "flower," "pink," "sun") and point to the corresponding objects or pictures in the storybook. "Listen: 'Look! The pink flowers!'" (Students point to the pink flowers). "Listen: 'A tall tree!'" (Students point to the tall tree).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice receptive listening for specific vocabulary and simple sentences. Grammar: Noun + verb + adjective (e.g., "The flowers are pink."), WH-questions (e.g., "What color is the flower?"), Present simple (e.g., "This is a tall tree."). Vocabulary: garden, flower, tree, plant, pink, green, tall, short, sun, grow.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>"Listen and Circle/Point" (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with pictures of a garden scene, featuring multiple flowers (some pink, some green), tall trees, short plants, and the sun. The teacher says a word or a short phrase (e.g., "pink flower," "tall tree," "green plant," "sun"), and students listen and circle or point to the correct picture on their worksheet. The teacher repeats each item twice slowly. This reinforces recognition of words and phonemic awareness (/s/ in "sun").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>"Teacher Says, You Point!" (Semi-controlled TPR):</b> The teacher places large flashcards of "garden," "flower," "tree," "plant," "pink," "green," "tall," "short," and "sun" around the classroom. The teacher gives instructions like: "Point to the pink flower!" "Point to the tall tree!" "Point to the green plant!" "Point to the sun!" Students listen and physically point to the correct flashcard. The teacher can then ask, "What color is this flower?" (pointing to a pink flower flashcard) and students point to "pink" flashcard or say "Pink."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>"Listen to Your Partner" (Pair Interaction):</b> Students work in pairs with a small set of picture cards (e.g., a pink flower, a green leaf, a tall tree, a short plant). Student A says, "Show me the pink flower!" (or "Point to the pink flower!"). Student B listens and points to the correct card. They then switch roles. The teacher circulates, observing listening comprehension and providing support.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "It's Our Garden."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Our Garden Tour" - A whole-class activity where students pretend to be exploring a garden. The teacher guides them with listening instructions and questions, and students respond with gestures, pointing, and very short, simple verbal responses (e.g., "Pink!").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. The teacher projects a large image of a garden or uses a large garden poster with various elements (pink flowers, green leaves, tall trees, short plants, sun).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. The teacher begins, "Welcome to our garden! Listen carefully!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. The teacher gives instructions for students to follow: "Look! Do you see a tall tree? Point to the tall tree!" (Students point). "What color are the flowers? (pointing to pink flowers). Are they pink?" (Students nod or say "Pink!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">4. The teacher continues with varied instructions and questions: "Find a green plant. Point to the green plant." "Is the sun big or small?" (Students point to "big" or gesture). "I like plants! Do you like plants?" (Students nod or thumbs up for "yes").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">5. The teacher encourages students to listen for key words and respond appropriately using gestures, pointing, or single-word answers (e.g., "Pink," "Tall"). This activity reinforces the pragmatic function of describing nature and asking questions.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Our Garden Tour" activity, focusing on individual student responses through pointing and gestures. A simple checklist can track which students correctly identify (by pointing) target vocabulary words like "flower," "tree," "pink," "tall," and "sun" when prompted.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will listen to the teacher's instructions/questions related to garden vocabulary and respond by pointing to large picture cards or a projected image of a garden.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher says: "Point to the pink flowers."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher says: "Do you see a tall tree?" (Students point to a tall tree and nod/thumbs up).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher says: "What color is this plant?" (pointing to a green plant in the picture; students point to green).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate identification (pointing/gesturing) of target vocabulary (e.g., "flower," "tree," "pink," "tall," "sun").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of response (clear pointing/gestures).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Engagement and participation in listening tasks.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Show a "happy face" and "sad face" emoji) "Did you hear 'flower' today? Point to happy face if yes, sad face if no."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Show a "thumbs up" and "thumbs down" image) "Did you like learning about the garden? Thumbs up or thumbs down?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new word you heard today about the garden?" (Students can point to a flashcard or a picture on a review sheet).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you show me a 'pink flower' with your hands or by pointing?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students successfully identified "flower," "tree," "pink," and "tall" through pointing. TPR and flashcards were highly effective for this age group. Some students struggled with differentiating "plant" from "bush," which will require more focused visual distinction next time. The "Our Garden Tour" activity generated good engagement. For the next lesson, I will ensure continued visual support and repeat listening exercises with the same core vocabulary. I need to prepare a song or rhyme that explicitly uses "green plant" and "bush" to reinforce those specific items.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson introduced core garden vocabulary through listening and visual recognition. The next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension) will focus on recognizing these same vocabulary words in a short, simple written text with illustrations, building on the auditory recognition established today. We will see the words "garden," "flower," "tree," "pink," and "tall" in print, linking sound to sight.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>